<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georg Forster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1754">1754</a>–<a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georg Forster was a German naturalist and writer who sailed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> on his second voyage and wrote a famous account of it. As librarian at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> he joined the Jacobin club and helped lead the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-german-national-convention">Rhenish-German National Convention</a> of the Republic of Mainz in 1793. He went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to seek union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and died there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georg-forster.jpg" alt="Georg Forster" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Heinrich Wilhelm Tischbein · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georg_Forster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Georg Forster was born on 27 November 1754 in Nassenhuben, a village near Danzig, the oldest of seven surviving children of the pastor and scholar Johann Reinhold Forster. At ten he accompanied his father on a 4,000 kilometre journey to inspect German colonies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> near Saratov, collecting hundreds of plant specimens. The family reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 4 October 1766, where the twelve-year-old translated Lomonosov's history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> into English.</p><p>In 1772 his father was invited by the Admiralty to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a>'s second expedition to the Pacific, and Georg went as his draughtsman. They sailed on HMS Resolution from Plymouth on 13 July 1772 and returned on 30 July 1775, having visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>, New Caledonia, Tahiti, the Marquesas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The elder Forster lost the right to write the official account, and Georg published his own, A Voyage Round the World, in 1777, six weeks before the official one. It described the societies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> with sympathy and without the noble savage romanticism that followed Bougainville's reports, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt">Alexander von Humboldt</a> later called Forster the founder of comparative ethnology and regional geography. He was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> on 9 January 1777, before the age of 23.</p><p>He taught natural history at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> from 1778 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> from 1784, and became head librarian at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> in 1788. He translated Cook's and Bligh's diaries, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> play Shakuntala, which influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Johann Gottfried Herder</a>. His Views of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-rhine">Lower Rhine</a>, published from 1791 to 1794, records a journey he made with Humboldt in 1790. In private letters from Vilnius he used insulting terms about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, which became known after his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>French troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> on 21 October 1792, and Forster helped found a Jacobin club there. The Mainz Republic, the first republic on German soil, existed until July 1793, and he was vice-president of its temporary administration. He and Adam Lux were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to ask that Mainz join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. While they were away Prussian and Austrian troops retook the city, and Forster, placed under the Imperial ban, stayed in Paris. He did not abandon the revolution during the Terror, and died of a rheumatic illness on 10 January 1794, at thirty-nine, while planning to visit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> he was long regarded as a traitor to his country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> named an Antarctic research station for him, opened on 25 October 1987, and the Alexander von Humboldt Foundation named a scholarship programme after him. The collections of the Forsters are held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> and at the Pitt Rivers Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georg Forster” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The convention, led by the professor Andreas Joseph Hofmann and the naturalist <b>Georg Forster</b>, abolished feudal rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-mainz">Republic of Mainz</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mokry Dwór, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.29,18.72&amp;year=1780" aria-label="Mokry Dwór on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4963 303 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Georg Forster"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5413 573h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5413 573h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.29,18.72&amp;year=1780">What was here in 1780? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
